# Windows local Python / openEMS (this machine)

When running `unittest`, `fairbeam` imports, or any check that loads `CSXCAD` / `openEMS`:

## Use these paths — do not rediscover

| What | Path |
| --- | --- |
| Repo venv Python | `d:\W\P\fairbeam\.venv\Scripts\python.exe` (from repo root: `.venv\Scripts\python.exe`) |
| `OPENEMS_INSTALL_PATH` | `C:\Users\ee\AppData\Local\org.fairbeam.desktop\gpu-runtime\openEMS` |
| Managed EMerge (Install EMerge) | `C:\Users\ee\AppData\Local\org.fairbeam.desktop\emerge\3.0.0b12\` (marker `fairbeam-emerge.json`) |

## How to run Python tests

```powershell
$env:OPENEMS_INSTALL_PATH = "C:\Users\ee\AppData\Local\org.fairbeam.desktop\gpu-runtime\openEMS"
cd d:\W\P\fairbeam\python
d:\W\P\fairbeam\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m unittest discover -s tests -p "test_emerge_*.py" -v
```

## Do not

- Search the disk / Everything / Prefetch for `openEMS.exe`, `CSXCAD.dll`, or random venvs.
- Use WindowsApps `python.exe` / `py -3` / Python 3.14 for Fairbeam tests (wrong or crashy NumPy/CSXCAD).
- Assume `C:\opt\openEMS` exists on this machine (docs example only; local install is the gpu-runtime folder above).

## If a path is missing

Stop and ask the human. Do not invent alternate roots or re-scan the filesystem.
